{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792161729,"nonce":"WIeNJcJYU8leDOYwLin+BT/2Ico=","title":"String constants retain excess UTF-16 capacity for the program's life","message":"crates/ash/src/bytecode.rs:1706, str_to_uchar_ptr does\n`s.encode_utf16().collect()`. Rust's collect() on an iterator whose\nsize_hint gives only a lower bound (EncodeUtf16's does, since UTF-16\ncode unit count is not knowable without full traversal for non-ASCII\ntext) grows the backing Vec by doubling, typically leaving spare\ncapacity above the final length. The result is never shrunk.\n\nThis runs once per string constant while loading a module's bytecode\n(read_string at line 320, called for field names, function names and\nabsolute names during the read pass), and the resulting Vec\u003cu16\u003e is\nretained for the program's whole life, so the wasted capacity is a\npermanent, cumulative cost proportional to the total size of a\nprogram's string constants. For an ashui-backed app, that is every CSS\nproperty and selector name, every error and debug string, and any large\nembedded stylesheet text (ashui's own user-agent stylesheet is one\nsizeable string constant among many smaller ones), potentially low\nhundreds of string constants.\n\nSince this is load-time, not a hot path, the fix costs nothing at\nruntime: add `.shrink_to_fit()` after `.collect()` in str_to_uchar_ptr.\nThe realloc happens once, at load, and the saved capacity is retained\nfor the rest of the run.\n\nSurfaced while investigating ashui's CodeWorkbench demo memory (ashui\ngit-bug e1a026f, 0973e0c). Not yet measured in isolation; the total\nsaving depends on how many string constants a typical ashui program\ncompiles in and how far collect()'s growth curve overshoots their\nlengths.","files":null}]}